### Background:

Wonderful job! Your witty and funny responses have everyone smiling and orders are getting delivered on time!

While doing your weekly reviews, you realized a new highly scalable solution is needed to store the order information for your application! You do your research and find a scalable NoSQL solution using Amazon DynamoDB. You will be testing the return item workflow where you'll be asking the retail order management chatbot for a replacement item!

### Your Task:

1. Create a DynamoDB table named "RetailOrderInventory" with the following attributes:
   - customer_id (partition key)
   - record_type_id (sort key)

2. Locate the "RetailOrderInventory" table you created earlier.

3. Click on the table name to open the table details.

4. Using the console, create an item and choose to enter "JSON" view.

7. In the JSON view, paste the following dummy customer data:

*  Insert the following data for a customer:

  {"customer_id": {
    "S": "9000004"
  },
  "record_type_id": {
    "S": "O3"
  },
  "email": {
    "S": "jane@gmail.com"
  },
  "location": {
    "S": "1234 main street"
  },
  "order_status": {
    "M": {
      "delivered_date": {
        "S": "12/20/2024"
      },
      "estimated_date": {
        "S": "11/14/2024"
      },
      "order_number": {
        "S": "1234561234561"
      },
      "status": {
        "S": "delivered"
      }
    }
  },
  "record_type": {
    "S": "customer_order"
  },
  "tracking_status": {
    "M": {
      "delivered_date": {
        "S": "12/16/2024"
      },
      "estimated_date": {
        "S": "12/16/2024"
      },
      "status": {
        "S": "dispatched"
      },
      "tracking_number": {
        "S": "123456789013"
      }
    }
  },
  "zip_code": {
    "S": "67890"
  }
}

8. In the DynamoDBImportFunction within AWS Lambda, add code to interact with the new DynamoDB table and store the order history. 
Insert &lt;code&gt;import os &lt;/code&gt; at line 3. Insert &lt;code&gt;orderdb = os.environ['dynamodb_tablename']&lt;/code&gt; at line 13 and replace line 14 with &lt;code&gt;
    table = dynamodb.Table(orderdb) &lt;/code&gt;
9. You'll have to identify how to provide dynamodb_tablename as a variable to the Lambda function.

10. Navigate back to the Lex chatbot and ask to 'return an item' and find replacement for order id for the customer and providing the zip code when asked. When asked to pick up or drop off, choose drop off and complete the task by entering the name of the store that the chatbot recommends!  

11. Enter 'not needed' when asked for the reason to return and ask for a replacement. Enter the zip code and choose to drop off at the location.

### Getting Started:

1. Create the DynamoDB table:

 * Use the AWS Management Console to create a new DynamoDB table named "RetailOrderInventory" with the following attributes:
customer_id (partition key)
record_type_id (sort key)

2. Insert the sample data:

 * Use the AWS Console to insert the provided JSON data for the customer into the DynamoDB table.

3. Integrate with AWS Lambda:

 * In your existing AWS Lambda function that handles the chatbot's intents, modify the necessary configuration to interact with the DynamoDB table and store the order history.

4. Test and Validate:

 * Scan the table to make sure the item entered exists, make sure you save the lambda code and deploy the function and finally remember to build the chatbot and test! 

### Inventory:

* Amazon Lex chatbot
* AWS Lambda Function 

Services You Should Use

* Amazon DynamoDB
* AWS Lambda
* Amazon Lex

### Task Validation:

Once the conversation is complete and a replacement is requested, enter the name of the store that the Lex Chatbot recommends to drop off the package at! '_ _ _  store'

&lt;h3&gt;Background&lt;/h3&gt;
Now that the notifications are being sent to the &lt;b&gt;desired targets&lt;/b&gt;, you need to ensure that developers receive only &lt;b&gt;messages&lt;/b&gt; specific to their &lt;b&gt;project&lt;/b&gt; (&lt;code&gt;project-name=jam-devops-msg-filter-proj&lt;/code&gt;).
&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Inventory&lt;/h3&gt;
 You will be using and making configuration changes to the following deployed resources  

1. &lt;b&gt;Lambda&lt;/b&gt; function &lt;code&gt;jam-devops-msg-filter-test-fn&lt;/code&gt; that is &lt;b&gt;subscribed&lt;/b&gt; to the &lt;b&gt;SNS&lt;/b&gt; topic &lt;code&gt;jam-devops-msg-filter-topic&lt;/code&gt; and simulates a developer receiving the messages.

Note: The attribute &lt;b&gt;"project-name"&lt;/b&gt; with value &lt;b&gt;"jam-devops-msg-filter-proj"&lt;/b&gt; should be used wherever required.
&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Getting Started&lt;/h3&gt;
You can use this documentation to find your way within the console:
   &lt;code&gt;https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/message-filtering-apply.html&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Services you should use&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;b&gt;SNS, Lambda, CodePipeline, CloudWatch&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Task Validation&lt;/h3&gt;
Once task is successfully completed, locate "Success Code:" in CloudWatch log group and paste it in validation box.

